Dear Make: Editors,

I would like to pitch a reproducible workshop project built around the
released Solidon3D 0.2.2, rather than a general software announcement.

Start with a downloaded bracket whose mounting hole is wrong. Solidon
recognises the existing hole in the STL, changes its diameter on the original
axis and adds a heat-set insert or bearing seat. The clearance is taken from a
small stepped gauge printed on the same machine with the same filament, not
from a generic tolerance table.

The article can answer three practical questions:

1. Does the edit preserve the position and angle of the original feature?
2. Does one measured gauge predict the fit of a different part?
3. Can a reader revise one value and reproduce the result without rebuilding
   the model?

Version 0.2.2 adds the direct hole edit, matching printable screws and nuts,
standards-based bearing seats and more capable in-viewport sketching. The
workflow does not require an account, cloud storage or the optional AI chat.

Overview: https://solidon3d.de/en/

I can supply a complete project kit: calibration and comparison models, a
measurement sheet, step-by-step images, final 3MF files and the released build
for Windows, macOS or Linux. There is no requirement to cover the software
positively—or at all.

